      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2949.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 691,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its fascinating aspects:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and hail. \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Found in the air as humidity, steam, and clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients in living things and in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite this vast amount, **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** (oceans). Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is endlessly recycled through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** No known life can survive without water. The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and cushions organs.\n*   **Habitat:** Millions of species of plants and animals live exclusively in water, from microscopic plankton to the blue whale, the largest animal on Earth.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that makes Earth unique, dynamic, and habitable."
